## Introduction

### What is Hadoop?
- **Hadoop** is an open-source framework for distributed storage and processing of large datasets
- Processes data in parallel across a cluster of computers
- Fault-tolerant and scalable
- Written in Java
- Part of the Apache ecosystem

### Historical Evolution
```
MapReduce paper (2003) → Hadoop 0.1 (2005) → Hadoop 0.20 (2009)
→ Hadoop 1.0 (2011) → Hadoop 2.0 (2013, introduces YARN)
→ Hadoop 3.0 (2017, containerization support)
→ Hadoop 3.3+ (2020+, improvements and security)
```

### Why Hadoop?
- **Scalability:** Scale from single server to thousands
- **Fault Tolerance:** Automatic replication and recovery
- **Cost-Effective:** Runs on commodity hardware
- **Flexibility:** Handles structured and unstructured data
- **High Throughput:** Optimized for batch processing
- **Data Locality:** Moves computation to data

### When NOT to Use Hadoop
- Real-time processing (use Spark Streaming, Kafka)
- Complex transactions (use traditional databases)
- Small datasets (< 100 GB, use standalone tools)
- Low-latency requirements
- Highly iterative algorithms (use Spark)

### Hadoop vs Other Technologies

| Feature | Hadoop | Spark | Kafka | Database |
|---------|--------|-------|-------|----------|
| Processing | Batch | Batch/Real-time | Real-time | Immediate |
| Speed | Slower | Faster | Real-time | Immediate |
| Cost | Cheap | Moderate | Moderate | Expensive |
| Fault Tolerance | Excellent | Good | Excellent | Good |
| Learning Curve | Steep | Moderate | Moderate | Easy |

