# Hadoop - Complete Concepts Reference with Theory & Examples

## 1. HDFS Block Storage - Complete Concept

### **Theory**

HDFS divides files into blocks and distributes them across DataNodes for parallel processing and fault tolerance.

```
File: data.txt (350 MB)
│
├─ Block 1 (128 MB) ────→ DataNode 1, DataNode 3, DataNode 5
├─ Block 2 (128 MB) ────→ DataNode 2, DataNode 4, DataNode 1
└─ Block 3 (94 MB)  ────→ DataNode 3, DataNode 5, DataNode 2
```

### **Key Theory Points**

| Concept | Explanation | Why It Matters |
|---------|-------------|----------------|
| **Block Size** | Default 128 MB or 256 MB | Larger blocks = fewer mappers, larger blocks = less metadata |
| **Replication** | 3 copies by default | Survives up to 2 node failures |
| **Rack Awareness** | 2 replicas in one rack, 1 in another | Balances fault tolerance and network efficiency |
| **Block Report** | DataNode sends every hour | NameNode knows block locations |
| **Heartbeat** | Every 3 seconds | NameNode detects dead nodes |

### **Example: File Placement Strategy**

```
File: transaction.log (512 MB)
3 blocks, replication factor 3

Block 1 (128 MB):
  Primary:   Node_A (Rack_1) ← Writer location
  Replica 1: Node_B (Rack_2) ← Different rack
  Replica 2: Node_C (Rack_1) ← Same rack, different node

Block 2 (128 MB):
  Primary:   Node_D (Rack_2)
  Replica 1: Node_E (Rack_1)
  Replica 2: Node_F (Rack_2)

Block 3 (256 MB):
  Primary:   Node_A (Rack_1)
  Replica 1: Node_C (Rack_1)
  Replica 2: Node_E (Rack_2)
```

### **Keypoints to Remember**

✓ **Block-level fault tolerance** - Individual block corruption doesn't affect entire file
✓ **Write-once model** - Blocks finalized after write completes
✓ **Block rebalancing** - Automatic if rack awareness violated
✓ **NameNode memory** - Each block = ~200 bytes in memory (1M blocks = 200 MB)
✓ **Under-replicated blocks** - Replication monitor schedules re-replication

### **Common Misconceptions**

❌ "Larger block size = better performance" → Only true if reduces mapper overhead
❌ "All 3 replicas on different racks" → No, wastes network bandwidth
❌ "NameNode stores blocks" → No, only metadata; DataNodes store actual blocks

