## 3. YARN Resource Allocation - Complete Concept

### **Theory: Container-Based Resource Management**

```
PHYSICAL NODE (16 GB RAM, 4 cores)
│
├─ NodeManager (200 MB)
│
└─ Allocatable Resources: 15.8 GB, 3.8 cores
    │
    ├─ Container 1: 2 GB, 1 core ──┬─ MapTask
    │                               └─ JVM
    │
    ├─ Container 2: 4 GB, 2 cores ──┬─ ReduceTask
    │                               └─ JVM
    │
    └─ Container 3: 2 GB, 1 core ──┬─ ApplicationMaster
                                    └─ JVM
```

### **Resource Request Flow**

```
ApplicationMaster
│
├─ Request 5 mappers: 1024 MB each, 1 core each
├─ Request 2 reducers: 2048 MB each, 1 core each
│
ResourceManager (Scheduler)
│
├─ Check available nodes
├─ Respect locality constraints
├─ Allocate on suitable nodes
└─ Notify ApplicationMaster

NodeManager
│
├─ Launch Container (isolated process)
├─ Monitor resource usage
├─ Kill if exceeds limits
└─ Report back to RM
```

### **Example: Resource Allocation Scenario**

```
Cluster Capacity:
├─ Node A: 16 GB, 4 cores
├─ Node B: 16 GB, 4 cores
├─ Node C: 16 GB, 4 cores
└─ Total: 48 GB, 12 cores

Minimum container: 1 GB, 1 core
Maximum container: 8 GB, 2 cores

Job Requirements:
├─ Mapper: 1024 MB, 1 core
├─ Reducer: 2048 MB, 1 core
└─ Total mappers needed: 10

Scheduler Decision:
Node A: 10 × 1024 MB = 10 GB (2 GB free, 4 cores available) → 8 mappers
Node B: 10 × 1024 MB = 10 GB (2 GB free, 4 cores available) → 8 mappers
Node C: 10 × 1024 MB = 10 GB (2 GB free, 4 cores available) → 6 mappers

Waiting for capacity or data locality improves with time
```

### **Keypoints to Remember**

✓ **Container = Isolated execution unit** - Own JVM, memory limit
✓ **NodeManager enforces limits** - Kills if exceeds allocated memory
✓ **Scheduler respects resource requests** - Must fit within container
✓ **Data locality matters** - Scheduler prioritizes NODE-LOCAL
✓ **CPU cores are soft limit** - Helps with scheduling but not enforced

