## 9. Job Configuration Tuning - Complete Concept

### **Theory: Configuration Impact**

```
Config Setting → Resource Allocation → Task Scheduling → Performance

Example:
mapreduce.map.memory.mb = 512 MB
│
├─ JVM Heap: 512 - 224 = 288 MB
├─ Sort Buffer: 288 * 0.1 = 28.8 MB
├─ Frequent spilling to disk
└─ Result: SLOW
```

### **Configuration Tuning Recipe**

```
STEP 1: Cluster Resources
├─ RAM per node: 16 GB
├─ Cores per node: 4
└─ Reserve for system: 2 GB

STEP 2: Calculate Container Size
Available: 16 - 2 = 14 GB
Containers: 7
Container size: 14/7 = 2 GB

STEP 3: Set Configuration
mapreduce.map.memory.mb = 2048
mapreduce.map.java.opts = -Xmx1600m

STEP 4: Monitor
Check: memory usage, job time, utilization
Adjust: iterate for optimal performance
```

### **Common Mistakes**

```
❌ java.opts (2GB) > memory.mb (1GB)
   Result: Out of memory

✓ java.opts (800MB) < memory.mb (1GB)
   Result: Works correctly

❌ Container size too small (256 MB)
   Result: Thousands of mappers, scheduling overhead

✓ Container size balanced (1-2 GB)
   Result: Reasonable parallelism
```

### **Keypoints to Remember**

✓ **224 MB overhead per container** - Account for it
✓ **Network >> CPU >> Disk** - Optimize in this order
✓ **Monitor actual usage** - Theory ≠ practice
✓ **No one-size-fits-all** - Iterate for your workload
✓ **Sort buffer critical** - Impacts spill frequency

